WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.624
John Wick: Chapter 4 is finally here, and
we've got some questions: Who is the Harbinger?

00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:07.117
What's the deal with Winston?

00:00:07.117 --> 00:00:10.600
And could this really be the end for everyone's
favorite assassin?

00:00:11.074 --> 00:00:16.039
The shocking end of John Wick: Chapter 3 saw
Winston shoot John Wick right off the roof

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:17.150
of the Continental.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:21.270
When John sees Winston again in Chapter 4,
however, he doesn't seem to have a problem

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:21.819
with him.

00:00:21.819 --> 00:00:26.250
So here's a question: when Winston shot John,
was he really shooting to kill?

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.870
Winston knows the High Table doesn't pull
its punches when it comes to punishment, so

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.330
it tracks that he really did try to kill John
to meet the Table's demands.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.360
Of course, it also tracks that Winston knows
his way around a bullet-proof suit — he's

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.250
also more than aware that John can take a
hit and fall off a building without going

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.342
down for the count.

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.701
"Very well, gentlemen.

00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:47.080
The Continental will now be reconsecrated."

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.780
There is also a clear bond between the two
men, and Winston even seems paternal towards

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.780
John at times.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.264
You can't help but think that he shot John
Wick to save his life rather than end it,

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:57.664
High Table be damned.

00:00:57.664 --> 00:01:01.550
For all of Winston's talk about rules being
what keeps a man separate from the animals,

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.790
he can't help being a man of instinct himself.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.080
While he defers to top dogs like the Adjudicator,
Winston stops at nothing to protect the rest

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.293
of his pack, even if he isn't always successful.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:17.040
John Wick sets out to bring down the High
Table in John Wick: Chapter 4, but he doesn't

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.260
start in New York.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.200
Instead, he heads to Morocco to chase down
the new Elder.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.880
But why does John kill this Elder?

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.170
In the third installment of the John Wick
franchise, John spends an eternity walking

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.290
through the desert to find the Elder.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:33.330
John's hope is that this mysterious figure
will reinstate him to the assassin's guild.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.510
The Elder demands John's ring finger in exchange
for removing the price tag on his head.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.740
In Chapter 4, John is back in the desert,
ready to kill this second Elder for not being

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.440
able to give him back the wedding ring the
old Elder took.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.260
The new Elder clearly doesn't know how to
read the room and tells John he'll only know

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.670
peace when he's dead.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.369
This leads to John killing him and sends the
High Table into punishment hyperdrive.

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:58.100
While this kill shot sets off the events of
the movie, it also speaks to the series' tragic

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.200
arc of revenge.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.280
The Elder has been replaced by a new Elder.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.649
The Marquis de Gramont will be replaced by
a new Marquis.

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:08.030
No matter how many Hydra heads or henchmen
John Wick takes down, new ones will spring

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.570
back up in their place, ready for a new fight
with the same old John Wick.

00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:15.850
The John Wick films are stuffed with symbolism
pulled from Greek and Roman mythology.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:20.630
It's a little weird, then, that Winston recalls
the mythic last words of a legendary Australian

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.930
outlaw in John Wick's last ride.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.989
Winston tells Charon that, after hearing he
was to be executed, the infamous Australian

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:28.394
bandit Ned Kelly said:

00:02:28.394 --> 00:02:29.564
"Such is life."

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.020
Clearly, these words mean something to Winston.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.850
To him, Ned's acceptance of his fate was noble
yet unsettling.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.690
Winston isn't ready to accept the fate handed
to him and his beloved Continental from the

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.849
High Table, however, nor does he accept that
John Wick should go down without a fight.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:47.650
Charon then utters the same phrase to Winston
before accepting his own fate.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.900
Even if audiences are shocked to see the Marquis
finish Charon off, this moment makes Winston

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.510
realize that the High Table will always take
more than he can give.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:03:01.050
When the Harbinger rolls up to the NYC Continental,
he is clearly ready to get down to business.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.680
But why is he missing a finger?

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.080
And why does that missing finger's nub suspiciously
twin with John Wick's?

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.160
Did the Harbinger try and fail to get out
of the High Table game, just like John Wick?

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.750
Is there some other reason he needed to cut
off a finger to prove his loyalty to the Table?

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.920
The Harbinger doesn't seem to take much pleasure
in enforcing the rules, unlike the smugly

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.989
evil Marquis.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:24.620
However, he doesn't back down from his duties
or try to get creative with the rules, unlike

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.580
Winston himself.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.860
So what's the story?

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.162
This man's backstory is one of the biggest
unanswered questions in John Wick: Chapter 4.

00:03:31.162 --> 00:03:33.360
The Harbinger's secret is all in his name.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.700
"Harbinger" is another word for a warning
of what is to come.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:40.819
Basically, he is John Wick's Ghost of Christmas
Yet to Come made flesh, here to show John

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:43.379
what he could become if he can't escape the
High Table.

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:47.549
The Harbinger was likely once an assassin
just like John Wick, one who, judging from

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:53.372
his demeanor and tell-tale finger, lived through
a similar story — yet suffered a very different fate.

00:03:53.372 --> 00:03:57.900
Obsessed with maintaining the supremacy of
the High Table, the villainous Marquis de

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.824
Gramont agrees to John Wick's terms for an
"old rules" duel and then quickly begins to cheat.

00:04:02.824 --> 00:04:06.540
He ups the bounty on John Wick's head and
sends his own assassins after him.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.069
The Marquis obviously has endless riches at
his disposal, but what's his story?

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:13.970
Why does he have so much power and so little
integrity?

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:18.963
The Marquis de Gramont represents how the
High Table's absolute power leads to absolute corruption.

00:04:18.963 --> 00:04:21.650
He worships the etiquette and ceremony of
the High Table.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.080
He is happy to exact the pounds of flesh and
pints of blood from those underneath him when

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.750
they break the rules, but when it's his time
to face the music, he chooses a proxy to fight

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.700
for him — and still tries to cheat his way
to victory.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.500
The Marquis is both a delightfully hateable
villain and a cautionary tale in the Wickverse.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.310
His power is orderly but ultimately corrupt,
superficial, and hollow.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.699
He demonstrates the dangers of standing on
ceremony instead of principal and favoring

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:49.590
excessive shows of power over actual skill.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.180
And that, in the end, is his downfall.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:55.180
"They gave you my name."

00:04:57.619 --> 00:04:58.619
"Yeah."

00:04:58.619 --> 00:05:02.630
In Chapter 4, the blind assassin Caine is
pressed into service to the High Table by

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.000
the Marquis de Gramont.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.120
Caine will do anything to keep his daughter
alive, well, and playing violin in the public

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.250
square — even if that means killing old
friend John Wick.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.380
So how does he actually manage this?

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.740
Okay, so this is less a statement of confusion
than it is an appreciation of Caine's creative

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.490
and relentless fighting style.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:23.970
For example, the slice of Caine's cane against
glass and steel is a compelling indicator

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.810
of the pain he's about to rain down on his
opponents.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.630
Meanwhile, Caine's heightened sense of hearing
helps him locate John Wick using the crunching

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.380
glass in the gallery scene at the Osaka Continental.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:38.696
However, one of Caine's most unique techniques
involves the use of cleverly placed doorbell sensors.

00:05:38.696 --> 00:05:42.940
At first, it's puzzling to see Caine slap
a bunch of electronic rectangles on various

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.960
surfaces in the Osaka Continental's dark kitchen.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.160
It almost looks like he's setting bomb charges,
but he isn't — he's just setting traps.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.280
When the High Table stormtroopers invade the
kitchen, they set off the doorbell sensors

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.380
and give Caine a precise audible target.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:57.540
What a badass.

00:05:57.845 --> 00:06:01.970
After John Wick kills the Elder, he seeks
refuge at Osaka's Continental.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:06.090
In the neon-lit night, John Wick seeks help
from yet another stern but loving assassin

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.590
hotel manager, Shimazu Koji.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.990
John Wick: Chapter 4 implies that John, Shimazu,
and Caine are old assassin friends.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:17.150
So why does Caine attack John Wick while Shimazu
risks everything to protect him?

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.690
The answer is simple: For Shimazu, friendship
is a matter of the heart — and when you're

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.410
in his heart, you're family.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.590
Caine draws the line between friend and family
to protect his daughter.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.900
His daughter means more to him than his friendship
with John Wick, and he refuses to defy the

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.580
High Table because he knows they will kill
her if he doesn't kill John Wick.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.400
Shimazu, meanwhile, knows the Table will come
for him and his daughter Akira merely for

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.340
harboring John Wick in their hotel.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:44.300
Shimazu fights just as hard for John Wick
as he does for his daughter, but his choice

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.370
to be loyal to his loved ones ultimately costs
him his life.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.430
Then again, considering the movie's post-credits
scene, it's possible that Caine's choices

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.055
will do the same for him.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.340
There are two things the Marquis loves more
than anything in this world: sparkly suits

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.710
and sending messages.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:02.750
The Marquis sends plenty of the latter after
John Wick defies the High Table, including

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:07.440
killing Pyotr, leader of the Ruska Roma, to
punish the crime family for John's actions.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.770
Pyotr might not ring any bells with viewers.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:14.870
Although he is an off-camera adoptive father
figure to John from back in his Jardani Jovanovich

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:19.250
days, the Director is the Ruska Roma character
that audiences are more likely to remember.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.780
Still, the death of Pyotr has left a bad taste
in the Ruska Roma family's mouth, and John's

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:28.520
adoptive sister Katia won't help John by sponsoring
his duel with the Marquis unless he kills

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.464
an enemy of hers: Killa Harkan.

00:07:30.464 --> 00:07:35.254
"It looks like we have ourselves a genuine
conundrum."

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:37.560
Killa is the baddie who pulled the trigger
on Pyotr.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:42.310
If John kills this boss, he gets branded back
into the Roma family for a seat at the Table

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.693
and a sanctioned shot at freedom by way of
dueling the Marquis.

00:07:45.693 --> 00:07:51.330
Mr. Nobody is one of the franchise's mysterious
newcomers, appearing out of nowhere as a fully-fledged

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:55.940
adversary to Mr. Wick, but is this man with
a plan John Wick's friend or foe?

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:00.479
At first, Mr. Nobody appears to be yet another
long-lost friend of John, emerging from the

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:03.560
shadows to pledge his sword and attack dog
to the cause.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.860
He fires a shot that saves John's life, but
it isn't because he shares Shimazu's brotherly

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.010
love for John.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:12.760
Nobody is trying to drive the price up on
John's head, not save it from rolling.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.610
While you can totally imagine a universe in
which Mr. Nobody and John buddy up — or

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:21.030
even one where Nobody finds a fistful of reasons
to take up John's fight against the High Table

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:25.259
— by the end of the film, Nobody is pursuing
a $40 million payday.

00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:29.530
That would pay for a whole lot of cargo pants,
sure, but why does he really need that much?

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:33.720
Well, Shamier Anderson recently told Inverse
it isn't Nobody that needs the cash at all.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:34.720
He said:

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.659
"Not to give away too much, but it was for
his mother."

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:40.580
John Wick: Chapter 4 seems to kill off John
Wick for good.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:45.029
After taking hit after hit and stair fall
after stair fall, John seems to meet his end

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:46.700
on the steps of Sacré-Coeur.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:50.899
He slumps to his side in the sunrise, enjoying
one last vision of the wife he lost.

00:08:50.899 --> 00:08:53.820
Later, the Bowery King and Winston visit John's
grave.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.709
He's buried next to his wife, and even though
the two visitors are a little lighthearted

00:08:57.709 --> 00:09:01.150
with their farewells, John's stone is inscribed
as he requested.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:05.400
So is this a clever set-up for an impossible
beginning in John Wick: Chapter 5, or is it

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.440
really the end?

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.320
The John Wick movies are such stunning spectacles
that it's easy to forget how much effort,

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.110
time, and talent they take to make.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:16.459
Four years passed between the release of John
Wick: Chapter 3 and its follow-up film, and

00:09:16.459 --> 00:09:21.610
Keanu Reeves and Chad Stahelski have plenty
on their plates aside from the world of Wick.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.850
The collaborators have been working together
in one way or another since their Matrix days.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.773
Who knows what their creative partnership
might yield if this is the end of John Wick's road?

00:09:28.773 --> 00:09:32.310
Although it seems like the character will
make an appearance in the Ballerina spin-off

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:36.950
movie, it really does seem like everyone's
favorite dog-avenging, grief-stricken husband

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.282
might be gone for good.

00:09:38.282 --> 00:09:39.195
Such is life.

